    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "The learner is stuck and needs a clear example to understand interval notation. I will provide a worked example with different numbers and then guide them to apply it to their problem.", "content": "Sure, let's start with a similar example. Suppose we have an inequality: -5 < x ≤ 3. In interval notation, this would be written as $x \\in (-5, 3]$. This means that $x$ can take any value greater than -5 (but not including -5) and up to and including 3. Now, can you try to write your inequality, m \in (-10; 8], in interval notation using this format?"},
    {"role": "user", "content": "So, for m \in (-10; 8], it would be m can take any value greater than -10 (not including -10) and up to and including 8. Is that right?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "The learner has correctly understood the concept and applied it to their problem. I will confirm their understanding and provide a breakdown of the formula and an exam tip.", "content": "Yes, that's correct! 🎉\n\n**Final Answer:** $m \\in (-10, 8]$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Format:** $m \\in (a, b]$\n* **What the letters mean:**
  * $a$: The lower bound (not included in the interval)
